/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a point inside a beam.
   - σ1 is the principal tensile stress; σ is the bending stress, positive in tension (N/mm²); τ = V Q/(I b) is the shear stress, with Q the first moment of the area beyond the point about the neutral axis (mm³), I the second moment of area (mm⁴
   - At the soffit τ = 0, so σ1 is horizontal and the crack is vertical.
   - Cut the beam along an inclined crack and look at the piece between the crack and the support.
   - Vcz is the shear in the uncracked compression zone; Va is the vertical part of aggregate interlock, friction between the rough crack faces; Vd is the dowel force in the main bars crossing the crack; Vs is the sum of the stirrup forces cross
   - Aggregate interlock works only while the crack is narrow, and dowel action is limited by the thin cover under the bars.
   - The truss analogy puts these together: the compression zone is the top chord, the tension steel the bottom chord, the concrete between cracks forms 45° struts and the stirrups are the vertical ties.
   - Without enough shear steel, the failure mode depends mainly on a/d, where a is the shear span (point load to support).
   - τv is the nominal shear stress (N/mm²); Vu is the factored shear force at the section (N); b is the width of the beam, or the web width bw of a flanged beam (mm); d is the effective depth (mm).
   - τc is the design shear strength of concrete (IS 456 Table 19).
*/
